<p>I work in commercial analytics for the pharmaceutical industry in Singapore, which mostly means reconciling data sources that describe the same reality and disagreeing about it. Deciding which number to trust, and being able to defend that decision in a room, is the actual job.</p>
<p>Rounding Errors is that job as a hobby. Countries publish numbers about themselves, other institutions publish different numbers about the same countries, and the gaps run to billions, occasionally an entire Greece. I collect the good ones.</p>
<p>Half the fun is the exhibits. The other half is seeing how far Claude and Claude Code can carry a one-person museum, currently: research, drafting, charts, and the entire deployment. Views here are mine and not my employer's.</p>

<p><b>How I run this</b></p>
<p>Exhibits get researched and drafted in conversation with Claude, where the arguing about tone happens. The draft goes to my laptop, where Claude Code builds it into the site: a content entry, a chart component, the wiring.</p>
<p>Then it gets checked. A fact-checker subagent runs in an isolated context on Sonnet, with no memory of writing the exhibit, and verifies every figure against its primary source. It is read-only: it can flag, never fix. It returns a verdict and a list of what it actually checked against.</p>
<p>It has already blocked a publication. Exhibit 006 claimed a finding that the cited GAO report does not contain. The fact-checker flagged it, the claim was traced to its real source, and the exhibit was corrected before anyone read it. That is on the <a class="inline" href={withBase('/corrections/')}>corrections page</a>.</p>
<p>Since August, every fact-checker verdict also goes to DeepSeek for an independent second opinion: same claim, same source, same retrieved passage, a different model with no memory of the first pass. The two don't always agree, and diffing exactly where they don't is its own small audit, written up in <a class="inline" href={withBase('/notes/')}>notes</a>.</p>
<p>Nothing deploys automatically. Every push is a decision I make.</p>
